ABATRACT: Herein, for the first time, we engineered click chemistry reaction to trigger 3D DNA walking machine for ultrasensitive electrochemical detection of copper ion $(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+})$ , which provided a convenient access to overcome the shortcomings of poor selectivity and limited amplification efficiency in the traditional determination of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ . Click chemistry reaction drove azido-S2 to bind with alkynyl-S1 for the formation of walker probe on aminated-magnetic polystyrene microspheres@gold nanoparticle $({\mathrm{PSC}}@{\mathrm{Au}})$ , which opened the hairpin-locked DNAzyme. In the presence of magnesium ion $(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+})$ , the unlocked DNAzyme was activated to cleave the self-strand at the facing ribonucleotide site, accompanying with the release of product DNA (S3) and the walker probe. Therefore, the walker probe was able to open adjacent hairpin-locked DNAzyme strand and then be released by DNAzyme cleavage along the PSC@Au-DNAzyme track. Eventually, the liberated single-strand S3 induced catalytic hairpin assembly (CHA) recycling, resulting in the capture of a large number of methylene blue-tagged hairpin DNA (MB-H2) on sensor surface and significant electrochemical responses. By coupling click chemistry reaction with the dual-amplification strategy of 3D DNA walking machine and CHA recycling, the proposed biosensor not only demonstrated high accuracy and selectivity for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ detection in real sample, but also showed excellent performance for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ detection with a wide linear range of $1.0~\mathrm{pM}$ to $500~\mathrm{nM}$ and low detection limit of $0.33\ \mathrm{pM}$ . Moreover, this elaborated biosensor could be readily expanded to $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ detection with a constant concentration of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , which paved a new way to apply the 3D DNA walking machine in various ions sensing.
## INTRODUCTION
Copper ion $(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+})$ , one of essential micronutrients in metabolic processes, has multiple functions for life, including electron transduction, enzyme catalysis and oxygen transport. Abnormal ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ levels in body would cause adverse health effects, such as copper deficiency diseases, liver and kidney damage, and even serious neurodegenerative diseases.1,2 It is therefore of great importance for human health to develop a simple, reliable and ultrasensitive method of monitoring and sensing the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ levels in water. To date, a variety of strategies have been developed for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ detection based on small organic molecules,3,4 nanomaterials5-7 and biomaterials.8-10 However, they are generally accompanied by some drawbacks of complicated organic synthesis steps, poor sensitivity and selectivity, and the use of toxic chemicals. Alternatively, click chemistry reaction has been used widely in sensing system due to its characteristic advantages including high selectivity, superior ligation efficiency and mild reaction conditions.11-14. Most reported ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sensors, however, without signal amplification strategies, suffer from relatively low detection sensitivity. To overcome this drawback, several reports of adopting multi-invertase conjugated magnetic bead signal amplification strategy15 or catalytic hairpin assembly (CHA)16 achieved sensitive detection of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ . Although the above studies improved the sensitivity of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ monitoring to some extent, the detecting sensitivity is still limited due to the lack of highly efficient amplification strategy, which cannot satisfy the requirement of trace detection of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ in the environmental and biological samples. Hence, it is desirable to explore a highly efficient amplification strategy for the ultrasensitive detection of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , which represents an advance over the traditional analytical methods.
DNA walking machine, one of intelligent and automatically ongoing DNA-based nanomachines, has been considered as an extremely attractive nucleic acid amplification method due to its inherent advantages of high cargoes loading capacity, accelerated target-recycling kinetics and the function of imitating protein motors.17,18 Powered by energy containing hybridization,19 hydrolysis20 and conformational change21,22 of DNA, DNA walking machine can walk progressively along the tracks to achieve cargoes transit and targets detection. However, most DNA walking machines were built on one-dimensional or two-dimensional tracks,23-25 which have the common limitation of low mobility and cargoes payload. Impressively, three-dimensional (3D) DNA walking machine, performing on 3D nanomaterials, possesses increased walking steps and satisfactory signal amplification of nucleic acids26 or proteins detection,27 because of the improved mobility of walker and high DNA loading efficiency.28,29 In this contribution, a 3D DNA walking machine triggered by click chemistry ligation between azido-modified DNA and alkynyl-modified DNA was proposed for ultrasensitive electrochemical detection of Cu2+.
In this work, we have for the first time applied click chemistry reaction to 3D DNA walking machine for developing a sensitive electrochemical biosensor of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ detection, which coupled the high selectivity of click chemistry reaction with dual-amplification strategy of 3D DNA walking machine and catalytic hairpin assembly (CHA) recycling. As shown in Scheme 1A, the prepared machine was comprised of azido-labeled S2 (azido-S2), alkynyl-labeled S1 (alkynyl-S1) and hairpin-locked DNAzyme30 track assembled on aminated-magnetic polystyrene microspheres $@$ gold nanoparticle $(\mathrm{PSC}@\mathrm{Au})$ by the Au-S bond. In the absence of target ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , the alkynyl- and azido-DNA were unable to bind for the hairpin-locked DNAzyme strand opening. Upon the addition of target ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$ was in-site generated through the reduction of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ under the help of sodium ascorbate (AA), which specifically and efficiently triggered the click chemistry between azido on S2 and alkynyl on S1, resulting in the formation of walker probe on PSC@Au. Then the walker probe caused an unfolding of hairpin-structured DNAzyme. In the presence of cofactor magnesium ion $(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+})$ , the unlocked DNAzyme was activated to cleave the self-strand at the ribonucleotide site, accompanying with the release of product DNA (S3) and walker probe from DNAzyme strand. Therefore, the walker probe was capable to open the adjacent hairpin-locked DNAzyme strand and then be released by DNAzyme cleavage along the PSC@Au-DNAzyme track. With the amplification strategy of 3D DNA walking machine, a large number of S3 were produced to trigger CHA recycling. Simultaneously, as displayed in Scheme 1B, thiol-labeled hairpin DNA (H1) was covalently attached to gold nanoparticles-assembled glassy carbon electrode (depAu/GCE) via Au-S bond. Next, mercaptohexanol (MCH) was employed to backfill the unspecific sites of electrode. When the obtained single-strand S3 was introduced onto the sensor, S3 could combine with H1 to open the hairpin structure of H1, generating a DNA duplex with an exposed tail. In this case, the exposed tail of H1 hybridized with the methylene blue-tagged hairpin DNA (MB-H2), triggering CHA recycling and releasing S3. The liberated S3 could thus bound with another MB-H2, and the reaction was switched on anew, achieving the attachment of plenty of MB-H2 onto the electrode with notable electrochemical responses. As a result, the coupling of click chemistry reaction with 3D DNA walking machine allowed the proposed biosensor toward ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ detection with high selectivity. With the dual-amplification strategy of 3D DNA walking machine and CHA recycling, this biosensor performed a high sensitivity for tracing ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ in real sample. Moreover, the elaborated biosensor could be employed for determination of $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ , which paved a way to applications of 3D DNA walking machine in various ions sensing.
