## Electrochemical Characterization of the Modified Electrode. To investigate the
stepwise fabrication process for modified electrode, CV and EIS were performed in PBS $\mathrm{~[0.1~M~}$ , containing 5 mM $\left[\mathrm{Fe(CN)_{6}}\right]^{3-/4-})$ . As shown in Figure 2A, relative to the bare GCE (curve a), modification of AuNPs caused the increasing redox peak current (curve b). The reason was that the AuNPs could promote the electron transfer between $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]}^{3-/4-}$ and the electrode. When the thiol-H1 was modified onto the sensing interface, however, the redox peak decreased (curve c), owing to the electrostatic repulsion between $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]}^{3-/4-}$ and the negatively charged phosphate backbones of thiol- $\mathrm{\cdotH}1^{34,35}$ . Moreover, the redox peak decreased again (curve d), upon the introduction of nonconductive MCH to block the nonspecific site of sensing interface. Undoubtedly, a further decreased redox peak was observed after incubating the mixture of MB-H2 and product S3 (curve e), owing to the fact that more negatively charged phosphate backbones have been introduced onto the electrode surface.
Figure 2B showed the impedance curves of stepwise modification process. Compared with the bare GCE (curve a), the value of electron-transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{et}})$ of depAu/GCE decreased (curve b) owing to the conductivity of the AuNPs. After negatively charged thiol-H1 (curve c) and nonconductive MCH (curve d) were sequentially modified onto electrode, however, the value of $R_{\mathrm{et}}$ increased continually. When the electrode was incubated with the mixture of MB-H2 and S3, the value of $R_{\mathrm{et}}$ increased greatly (curve e). These results indicated the successful fabrication of modified electrode according to Scheme 1.
electrolyte: (a) bare GCE, (b) depAu/GCE, (c) thiol-H1/depAu/GCE,
MCH/thiol-H1/depAu/GCE, and (e) MB-H2/MCH/thiol-H1/depAu/GCE.
Optimization of the Experimental Conditions. Because the amplification efficiency of 3D DNA walking machine relies on both the click chemistry reaction and the $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ -dependent DNAzyme cleavage, the concentration ratio between S1 and DNAzyme was optimized in this study. To investigate the optimal ratio, the proposed biosensor was evaluated by using SWV with six ratios (from 1:5 to 1:30). With the increase of ratio from 1:5 to 1:20, the current response increased and reached a plateau after 1:20. Therefore, 1:20 was chosen as the optimal ratio for subsequent experiments (Figure 3A).
Under the above optimal condition, the click chemistry reaction time was also optimized at room temperature. The proposed biosensor was studied by SWV in the presence of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\left(100\mathrm{nM}\right)$ at a time interval of 15 min (0, 15, 30, 45, 60, 75, 90, 105 min). As shown in Figure 3B, the SWV response increased rapidly with prolonged reaction time and the tended to level off after 90 min. Therefore, 90 min was enough for click chemistry. We also used polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis to examine the click chemistry time, and similar results were obtained (Figure S1).
