## Analytical Chemistry
Specificity, Reproducibility and Stability of the Proposed Biosensor. To further evaluate the specificity, ${\mathrm{Ag}}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ $(10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , respectively) were chosen as interfering ions, whose concentration was hundred-fold relative to the concentration of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(100\ \mathrm{nM})$ . As shown in Figure 6, compared with the blank control, no significant SWV responses of interfering ions were observed except that of target $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(100\ \mathrm{nM})$ . In addition, while $100\ \mathrm{nM}\ \mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ coexisted with all of interfering ions $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , respectively), the SWV response of mixture was not almost changed in comparison with the case of only target ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ . These results indicated good specificity of this strategy for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ detection. The reproducibility of the biosensor was studied by inter-assay (the same electrode toward $100\mathrm{\nM\Cu}^{2+}$ was detected for five times) and intra-assay (five electrodes were performed with $100\ \mathrm{nM}\ \mathrm{Cu}^{2+},$ ). The relative standard deviation (RSD) of inter-assay and intra-assay was $4.2\%$ and $6.1\%$ , respectively, demonstrated that the reproducibility was acceptable. The stability was monitored by storing the biosensor at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and assaying every 3 days by SWV. After 15 days, the SWV response retained
$90.6\%$ of its initial current, suggesting that the biosensor had accepted stability.
different interferences: ${\mathrm{Ag}}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ , $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ . The concentration of each interfering ion was $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , which was hundredfold than target ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ (100 nM).
Recovery Test. To investigate the application potential of the developed biosensor, a recovery experiment was adopted. Briefly, the tap water samples, obtained from the tap in our laboratory, was used to prepare various concentrations of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ . From Table 2, we could see the recovery ranged from $98.8\%$ to $105\%$ and RSDs varied from $2.2\%$ to $9.4\%$ , suggesting our proposed strategy was promising for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ detection in tap water.
