## CONCLUSION
In conclusion, this work has developed a high selective and ultrasensitive approach for electrochemical detection of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ by coupling the click chemistry reaction-triggered 3D DNA walking machine with CHA recycling, which performed attractive features over the traditional detection of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ . Click chemistry reaction was applied for the first time to initiate 3D DNA walking machine, which allowed the proposed biosensor toward ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ detection with high selectivity. With the dual-amplification strategy of 3D DNA walking machine and CHA recycling, the sensitivity of the designed biosensor was greatly enhanced. Interestingly, it would be readily extended for detecting $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ , offering great potential for the detection of various ions in diverse fields such as clinical diagnosis and environmental area.
